  "sources": [
    {
      "title": "ACEdit: Stable Multi-Turn Image Editing via Asymmetric Conditioning",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22328",
      "description": "arXiv:2610.22328v1 Announce Type: cross \nAbstract: Image editing models produce impressive results for individual edits, but their quality often deteriorates over multiple editing turns. Each editing turn introduces small distribution shifts that accumulate over successive edits, creating a gap between the input distributions encountered during the editor's original training and multi-turn inference. We introduce a training scheme that makes multi-turn image editing stable under this accumulating distribution shift. A key is to force the editor to operate on its own outputs from previous editing turns during training, bringing the principle of Self-Forcing to image editing. The main challenge is that self-generated editing trajectories deviate from ground-truth trajectories, making direct supervision unreliable. We address this challenge through a distillation objective that leverages a teacher image editor to provide a reliable training signal when the model operates on its own previous outputs. We introduce an asymmetric conditioning scheme: while the student performs edits sequentially on its own outputs, the teacher remains anchored to the original clean image and receives a compound editing instruction. Experiments show substantially reduced degradation over long multi-turn sequences while maintaining editing quality and instruction adherence.",
      "source_name": "arXiv AI",
      "published": "2026-10-16T04:00:00"
    },
    {
      "title": "VDE Bench: Evaluating The Capability of Image Editing Models to Modify Visual Documents",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.00122",
      "description": "arXiv:2602.00122v4 Announce Type: replace-cross \nAbstract: Image editing models can modify visual content through natural language, but editing dense documents requires precise local changes amid tightly coupled text, tables, and layout. We introduce VDE Bench, an evaluation resource for studying instruction-guided editing of Chinese and English document images. VDE Bench contains 3,506 instruction-guided image editing samples, including 1,578 text-region edits and 1,928 table-region edits. The benchmark pairs editing instructions and reference images with annotated editing regions and structured OCR outputs. Its evaluation protocol combines region-level and full-page measurements to assess textual correctness, structural fidelity, and visual content preservation. By focusing on the interaction between local edits and their surrounding document context, VDE Bench complements existing visual text editing benchmarks and provides a resource for diagnosing document-specific editing failures.",
      "source_name": "arXiv AI",
      "published": "2026-10-16T04:00:00"
    }
  ]
